hub.com/users/lyuma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lyuma/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The code looks like it should work.\r\n\r\nFor overrun it seems reasonable, basically skipping ahead if the mic is faster than the system. I'm a little worried that this is not solving the underlying problem, but just causing godot to lose a packet every so often combined with very high increasing delay.\r\n\r\nAlso for the case where the microphone is slower than the system clock (underrun), this could cause it to loop back and play old audio for some time (while jumping the delay up). I wonder if it would be better to fill in zeros (or smear the last output sample / something else) in the case that we underrun the mic buffer. I realize that looping created symmetry in the > and < cases, but I think looping is not a good way to handle underrun.","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/114094#pullrequestreview-3629691911","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/114094","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/114094#pullrequestreview-3629691911"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/114094"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-06T07:14:43Z","commit_id":"2eaa3f09bc3e43b04b6a00afbfea487a560c28e3"},{"id":3629759287,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOAO6SJc7YWbs3","user":{"login":"lyuma","id":39946030,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM5OTQ2MDMw","avatar_url":"https://avata